    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We propose to develop a methodology for predicting the geometric and hydraulic characteristics of subsurface brittle fault networks formed by a sheared joint mechanism. First, we investigate the process of fault segment linkage by examining how through-going slip surfaces breach relays to connect formerly independent faults or isolated fault segments. Second, we examine the relationship between the lengths of slip surfaces which define effective fault segments along faults of different length-scale and maximum slip. We suggest that these two data sets together with a geomechanical model of splay fracture angle or fault intersection angle could provide a means to understand the development of fault networks and improve our capability to predict the geometry, continuity, and density of faults and fractures in subsurface networks. Superb exposure of fault zones in a well-developed network in the Valley of Fire presents an exceptional opportunity to quantify the process of fault growth by the linkage of segments and the accompanying development of a fault core.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
